reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
WHERE CHANCE COMES IN. There are two sources:
- the favour deal, which is symmetric in 3-4 player games because the goods are distinct and identical in value;
- the daily shuffle, whose revealed cards are public and open to every bidder equally.
The main luck is whether cards of your favoured good happen to come up. That is worth about 3 per card, typically 0-15 a day, against prize and price swings of 10-40 a day. Estimated share of the result due to chance: about 20-30%.

WHEN EVERYONE PLAYS WELL. Results are decided by:
- bid sizing (shading in a first-price auction while avoiding void ties);
- budgeting florins across three days;
- timing majority races (cumulative counts pay three times);
- ship ranking.
The deal alone does not settle the winner.

INFERENCE. Rivals' favoured goods can be inferred from their bids on mixed lots and from the active player's lot cuts. In 3-4 player games the favoured goods are distinct, so one player's good rules it out for the others. Rivals' remaining florins can be tracked almost exactly from public prices. The information can be acted on by underbidding or overbidding, by cutting the lot to deny a rival, and by contesting majorities. A reasoning player is clearly favoured over a random or thoughtless one, as the policy tests above show.
